they clamber down the canyon wall  driven by the instinct to survive  tens of thousands have made this journey  in the past few weeks  a desperate voyage made by desperate  people from the ethiopian state  of tigre  but they must cross this torrent in an  overloaded  boat if they wish to complete it  there is a constant flow of refugees now  making their way down to the river  and for many this will be the end of a  very long  journey but they've got one more  obstacle  to face the takisei river  forms a natural frontier between  ethiopia  and neighboring sudan and at least 30  000 refugees have decided to cross it  it is a momentous decision that many  have taken  in an instant  [Music]  they took our clothes our home our money  they took it all  we don't have anything we came here  naked just like this  this isolated spot filled with frenetic  activity as ethnic tigrayans flee  bombing  shelling and persecution and the  situation  may deteriorate the ethiopian prime  minister ordering the leaders of tigre  state  to surrender but few expect them to do  so  for some the experience is shattering  [Music]  i wish i could go home said this woman  it took her ten days to walk to the  river  we took a boat over to the ethiopian  side  to see whether refugees were gathering  we're in ethiopia now integra state  and uh well it's chaos here  there is a customs post and a small town  called dima  a few minutes in land and we decided to  make a short visit  we saw refugees sheltering from the sun  in dozens of homes and compounds  and sudanese military sources told us  there are thousands more on the way  they will find sanctuary in the form of  a sudanese camp  but first they must cross the takezi and  we traveled alongside them  in a leaky metal tub  [Applause]  [Music]  however everyone we spoke to said they  were willing to take the risk  for life in tigre has become unthinkable  it took 12 days to get here we lived in  a forest  a forest but now we have reached sudan  find a tent at the top of the hill where  they will collect a wristband with a  barcode  entitling them to basic provisions and a  blanket  the start of something unfamiliar many  miles from home  it's going to go on for a long time yeah  i think it will be heartbreaking  your heart's breaking okay  [Music]  they may be here for months or they  could be here for years  as a full-scale humanitarian crisis  unfolds  john sparks sky news in hamdayet eastern  sudan
 

